#: The vendor library, bound by `the_library()` at the moment it is first needed.
#:
#: **Never imported at module scope**, because `--capabilities` must answer on a machine where
#: nothing is installed: it is asked before a channel exists, offline, and an adapter that cannot say
#: what it can do until its dependency is present is one rundesk cannot ask.
slack_sdk = None
SocketModeClient = None
SocketModeResponse = None
WebClient = None
WebhookClient = None
SlackApiError = None

#: What Slack takes in one message. **Under its own recommendation on purpose**: Slack advises four
#: thousand characters and truncates hard at forty thousand, and the room between the two is what
#: pays for escaping — `&` becomes `&amp;` on the way out, so text checked at this number cannot
#: reach the hard limit however much of it is reserved punctuation.
MAX_TEXT = 3800

#: What this can do, asked with no network and no account, and the same answer every time.
#:
#: **Honest about being quiet.** Nothing here streams progress and nothing here edits a message it
#: has posted, so both say so rather than claiming an ability the surface deliberately does not use.
#: `react` and `thread` are real: a turn is marked on the message that asked for it, and a mention in
#: a channel is answered in a thread of its own.
#:
#: `attach` is `True` in both directions. Out: a file the agent declared is verified again and
#: uploaded into the exact conversation the answer went to (R-SLK-47). In: a file somebody attached
#: to a message that wakes the agent is landed as it arrives, the way the Discord adapter lands one
#: (R-SLK-66), and `fetch` brings in the files of one message a search found (R-SLK-58).
#:
#: `search` is `True` because this really looks, and it is the one key rundesk acts on: it is read
#: offline and with no credential, and a channel that does not say `True` here is never asked to
#: search at all. **Honest about how it looks**: Slack publishes no search a bot token may call, so
#: what stands behind this key is enumerating the conversations this bot was invited to and reading
#: their history — bounded, and saying in `partial` wherever it stopped.
#:
#: `max_text` is the constant above and never a second copy of it.
#: **`address` is what lets a schedule report somewhere of its own.** rundesk holds no Slack
#: credential, so it cannot turn a user id into the direct conversation that user reads — that is
#: `conversations.open`, a call only this side can make — and the string this adapter composes for a
#: place is its own and never rundesk's to build. Saying so is what allows rundesk to accept `--to`
#: for this channel; an adapter that says nothing has `--to` refused where somebody typed it.
CAPABILITIES = {"stream": False, "edit": "none", "react": True, "thread": True, "attach": True,
                "address": True, "search": True, "max_text": MAX_TEXT}

#: Where the two credentials are read from. Slack needs both and one without the other reaches
#: nothing: the app-level token opens the socket, the bot token calls the Web API. **Never an
#: argument** — a command line is readable through the process list and lands in a shell's history —
#: and never a file: what rundesk keeps is these names, and the value arrives under each of them.
BOT_TOKEN_FROM = "SLACK_BOT_TOKEN"
APP_TOKEN_FROM = "SLACK_APP_TOKEN"

#: How Slack spells each kind of token, and the one this must never be handed.
#:
#: **A user token is refused by name.** `xoxp-` is a *person's* token: it carries that person's own
#: view of the workspace — every direct message they can read, every channel they are in — and an
#: agent holding one would be reading their mail rather than being spoken to. The prefixes are
#: checked before any call, because the refusal has to say which mistake was made and `auth.test`
#: answers `ok` for all three.
BOT_TOKEN_STARTS = "xoxb-"
APP_TOKEN_STARTS = "xapp-"
USER_TOKEN_STARTS = "xoxp-"

#: The bot scopes **`--check` refuses over**, each against something in this file. **Short on
#: purpose**: a scope granted for nothing is a scope somebody was asked for under false pretences.
#: There is no `search:read`, no `users:read.email`, and nothing that would let this read a channel
#: it has not been invited to.
#:
#: **This set is what makes a channel unreachable, so what goes in it is the owner's decision.** The
#: three scopes that only widen what an unscoped `search` can list are in `FURTHER_SCOPES` and are
#: read at runtime instead, because a channel is whole without them. `files:read` is here on the
#: owner's word (R-SLK-66): a file somebody attaches to a message is landed the way the Discord
#: adapter lands one, and an app that cannot read one is a channel that drops what people send it —
#: so it is refused at `--check`, and the cost is one reinstall per app installed before it existed.
WANTED_SCOPES = {
    "app_mentions:read": "being named in a channel",
    "chat:write": "answering, and setting the agent session's status while a turn runs",
    "channels:history": "reading the thread a mention stands in, in a public channel",
    "groups:history": "the same, in a private channel it has been invited to",
    "channels:read": "naming the channel an answer is being written in",
    "groups:read": "the same, for a private channel",
    "im:history": "reading a direct message",
    "im:write": "opening the direct message unprompted things are written to",
    "reactions:write": "👀 while a turn runs, and ✅ once it has answered",
    "users:read": "saying who spoke, in the words Slack shows rather than as an id",
    "files:write": "uploading a file the agent attached to its answer",
    "files:read": "landing a file somebody attached to a message, and downloading one a search found",
}

#: What a turn looks like on the exact message that asked for it, and the whole of it.
#:
#: **`working` has no mark**, because it has Slack's own agent-session status instead — a mark for it
#: would say a message had been seen twice. **`stopped` and `failed` have none either**, and that is
#: deliberate rather than missing: the answer rundesk delivers for either of them is the news, and a
#: second glyph beside it is this file having an opinion about a turn it did not run. What they do is
#: take the 👀 down, so nothing is left wearing the mark for a turn that ended.
MARKS = {"seen": "eyes", "done": "white_check_mark"}
SEEN = "seen"
WORKING = "working"
ENDED = ("done", "stopped", "failed")

#: Slack's own status for an agent that is working, and the one it is put back to when the turn
#: settles. **Set through `agents.sessions.setStatus`**, which is the current method: the older
#: `assistant.threads.setStatus` and the streaming methods are deliberately not used.
#:
#: **A session takes one of four words and these are two of them** — `active`, `processing`,
#: `suspended`, `closed`. The empty string is not a third: it clears the *older*
#: `assistant.threads.setStatus`, and this method answers it with `invalid_status`. Nothing here
#: sends a status that is not one Slack documents, and a settled turn is `active` and nothing else.
#:
#: It needs a granular bot token carrying `chat:write`, the app declared as an agent in its own app
#: settings, the bot to be a member of the channel, and both `channel_id` and `thread_ts` — so there
#: is no session for a direct message that is not in a thread, and none at all for an app that has
#: not been declared. Some AI features need a paid plan, and a workspace guest cannot reach an agent
#: app at all. Each of those is a *documented absence* here rather than something to work around:
#: when the status cannot be set, nothing is shown, and on no account is a chat message posted to
#: imitate it.
WORKING_STATUS = "processing"
SETTLED_STATUS = "active"

#: The four a session may be in, held so that nothing here can send a fifth. Read by the one call
#: that makes a request, because a status Slack has never heard of is a request worth not making.
EVERY_STATUS = frozenset({"active", "processing", "suspended", "closed"})

#: What Slack answers when the whole mechanism is unavailable to this app in this workspace.
#: **Only what its own reference says is about the token, the app or the method** — one of these
#: proves that no session will be set anywhere, so the status is put down until this adapter is
#: started again:
#:
#:     feature_disabled          "The agent tasks feature is not enabled for this workspace."
#:     missing_scope             "The token used is not granted the specific scope permissions
#:                                required to complete this request."
#:     not_allowed_token_type    "The token type used in this request is not allowed."
#:     team_access_not_granted   "The token used is not granted the specific workspace access
#:                                required to complete this request."
#:     method_deprecated         "The method has been deprecated."
#:     deprecated_endpoint       "The endpoint has been deprecated."
NO_SESSIONS_AT_ALL = frozenset({
    "feature_disabled", "missing_scope", "not_allowed_token_type", "team_access_not_granted",
    "method_deprecated", "deprecated_endpoint",
})

#: What Slack answers about **the one channel or thread this call named**, and about nothing else.
#: Each of these is quoted from the same page and each names the argument it is about, so a refusal
#: here suppresses that one session and can never reach a place it was not about:
#:
#:     channel_not_found      "The specified channel does not exist or is not accessible."
#:     not_authorized         "The caller is not a member of the specified channel."
#:     no_permission          "…Make sure your app is a member of the conversation it's attempting
#:                             to post a message to."
#:     thread_ts_required     "`thread_ts` is required for thread-based sessions in regular
#:                             channels and DMs."
#:     thread_ts_not_allowed  "`thread_ts` must not be provided for session channels…"
#:
#: **Everything else is one call that failed and nothing more.** `fatal_error`, `internal_error`,
#: `request_timeout`, `service_unavailable`, `access_denied`, `invalid_arguments`, `accesslimited`
#: and anything this release has never heard of are reported where they happen and suppress
#: nothing: none of them is Slack saying this app cannot do this, and guessing that it is would
#: switch an agent's indicator off over a bad moment.
NO_SESSION_HERE = frozenset({
    "channel_not_found", "not_authorized", "no_permission", "thread_ts_required",
    "thread_ts_not_allowed",
})

#: The one word Slack attaches a number to. **It is never retried inline** — see `_rate_limited`,
#: which turns it into a period during which no status call is made at all.
RATE_LIMITED = "ratelimited"

#: The Slack method that sets it, named once. Called through `api_call` rather than through a
#: generated method so that the pin in `requirements.txt` does not have to be the release that first
#: carried a binding for it; the wire name is the contract either way.
STATUS_METHOD = "agents.sessions.setStatus"

#: The two events this subscribes to, which is the whole of the wake rule. **Nothing subscribes to
#: `message.channels` or `message.groups`**, so an unmentioned message in a channel or in a thread
#: produces no event — it is not filtered out here, it never arrives.
MENTION = "app_mention"
MESSAGE = "message"
HELLO = "hello"
EVENTS_API = "events_api"

#: The envelope a slash command arrives in. **Not an event, and never read as one**: it carries no
#: `event` object, it is answered rather than recorded, and Slack sends an app only the commands
#: that app itself declares — so whatever arrives here is this agent's own.
SLASH = "slash_commands"

#: Content-free diagnostic boundaries a decoded envelope reaches. Each is said once per run, as a
#: note record, which is what puts it in the agent's own log where `rundesk gateways logs` shows it.
#: **stderr is not that place**: the gateway copies a bounded tail of an adapter's error stream into
#: the log when it collects one that has already exited, and never while it is running — so a
#: boundary written there is invisible for as long as the channel is working, which is exactly when
#: somebody is asking why nothing arrives.
IGNORED = {
    "not_an_event": "Slack delivered an envelope that was not an events_api event",
    "no_event": "Slack delivered an events_api envelope without an event object",
    "not_woken": "Slack delivered an event this adapter does not wake for",
    "already": "Slack redelivered an event this adapter had already handled",
    "not_allowed": "Slack delivered an event outside the configured sender and place allow lists",
    "woken": "Slack delivered an allowed event that woke this channel",
    "no_command": "Slack delivered a slash command with nothing in it this could act on",
    "not_theirs": "Slack delivered a slash command from outside the configured sender and place "
                  "allow lists",
    "not_a_gesture": "Slack delivered a slash command naming something this adapter does not offer",
    "commanded": "Slack delivered a slash command that reached this agent",
    "already_commanded": "Slack redelivered a slash command this adapter had already acted on",
}

#: The same, one step earlier: what arrived on the websocket itself, before anything was decoded.
#: **Slack's own type string is never repeated**, so a frame carrying a private payload cannot put
#: any part of it in a log: anything this release has no word for is `other`. `hello` has a sentence
#: of its own below and is not named here twice.
#:
#: This is the boundary the decoded ones above cannot stand in for. An adapter that says nothing
#: between `hello` and a turn leaves silence meaning two different things — Slack sent nothing, or
#: Slack sent something this did not decode — and no evidence tells them apart.
#:
#: **Two words this deliberately does not have.** There is none for `disconnect`, because the vendor
#: client acts on one and returns before any message listener is called. There is none for malformed
#: input either: that client parses the frame itself, hands on a mapping and nothing else, and gives
#: an empty one for anything that was not JSON — which arrives here as `other`, the same as any
#: other frame this has no word for. Either would be a sentence that can never be said.
ARRIVING = {
    EVENTS_API: "Slack sent an events_api frame on this Socket Mode websocket",
    SLASH: "Slack sent a slash_commands frame on this Socket Mode websocket",
    "other": "Slack sent a frame of another kind on this Socket Mode websocket",
}

#: The one control that cannot be undone, and how long somebody has to ask for it a second time.
#: **A gateway shut down from here cannot be started from here**: the bot goes offline and there is
#: no command left. One mistyped subcommand is the failure this prevents.
SHUTDOWN = "shutdown"
CONFIRM_WITHIN = 30.0

#: The gestures this one command offers, as its subcommands (R-SLK-37). Each is `(the word somebody
#: types, what the help beside it says, the word rundesk speaks)`, and **the third is never the
#: first**: `new` is what a person is offered and `forget` is what rundesk calls it, because *new*
#: says what they get and *forget* says what happens to the session. A word here rundesk does not
#: know is a subcommand that is offered, typed, and does nothing — so the suite checks these against
#: rundesk's own closed list rather than trusting them to match it.
#:
#: **What one is answered with is what rundesk said about it and never what it did.** A `stop` where
#: no turn is running, and a `new` in a conversation that has none, are both ordinary — and the
#: sentence saying so is rundesk's, which is why nothing here claims an effect of its own.
CONTROLS = (
    ("stop", "Stop the turn running here", "stop"),
    ("new", "Start a new session — the next message begins fresh", "forget"),
    ("restart", "Restart this agent's gateway", "restart"),
    ("shutdown", "Shut this agent's gateway down for good", SHUTDOWN),
)

#: The read-only questions. The names are what somebody types; the values are rundesk's closed
#: vocabulary, so this never becomes a way to run something arbitrary. Each is answered out of what
#: the install already knows and **never by starting a turn**, so an answer costs nobody's tokens.
QUERIES = (
    ("status", "Show this agent's gateway status", "status"),
    ("version", "Show the rundesk version this agent is running", "version"),
    ("agents", "List every agent and the skills each holds", "agents"),
    ("skills", "List the skills this agent holds", "skills"),
    ("schedules", "List what this agent still has to run", "schedules"),
    ("delegations", "Show delegated work relevant to this conversation", "delegations"),
)

#: The one subcommand that takes a value, and the one that changes an agent-wide default. Rundesk
#: refuses it on a channel more than one person may reach, which is its decision and not this file's.
CONFIGURE = ("provider", "Change which brain answers for this agent")

#: How a private answer goes back: Slack's own response url for that one command, as an **ephemeral**
#: response every time. A command typed in a channel is answered to whoever typed it and never into
#: the channel, so an answer naming this install's agents cannot be read by the room it was asked in.
EPHEMERAL = "ephemeral"

#: How many answers Slack takes on one command's response url. Its own published allowance, held here
#: so a long answer is bounded by the platform's rule rather than by what this file hopes.
RESPONSES_MOST = 5

#: What is said when Slack refused one piece of a private answer. A visible warning is the difference
#: between a partial install-wide directory and one mistaken for the whole of it.
INCOMPLETE = "⚠️ This private answer is incomplete. Please run the command again."

#: What is said instead of the last piece of an answer longer than Slack's own allowance holds.
#: **Said rather than dropped**, because a list cut off where nobody was told is a list somebody
#: reads as complete — and `rundesk` on the machine itself answers the same question without a bound.
TOO_LONG = ("⚠️ The rest of this answer is past what Slack shows on one command. Ask the machine "
            "this agent runs on for the whole of it.")

#: What is said, once per run, the first time rundesk sends something the agent said on its way to
#: an answer. **The suppression is the surface working, not a fault** — but it is invisible from
#: Slack, and an owner comparing what a brain wrote with what a channel shows has to be able to see
#: that the difference was deliberate. Rundesk composes an answer for this surface out of every
#: finished thought, so what is dropped here is a delivery whose words are in that answer already.
NOT_THE_ANSWER = ("rundesk sent something the agent said before its answer; this surface shows the "
                  "answer alone, so it was not posted")

#: Whether the websocket and the bot token belong to one Slack app, said as a verdict and never as
#: an id. **Two tokens from two apps is the failure this names**: both are valid, `auth.test`
#: passes, the app-level token opens a socket and Slack greets it — and every event the bot is
#: subscribed to goes to the *other* app's connections, so the agent is reachable by nothing while
#: reporting itself connected. `bots.info` answers the bot token's app and Slack's own `hello`
#: answers the socket's, and neither id is ever written down or logged.
#:
#: **All three are reports and none of them decides anything.** The comparison has never run against
#: a live workspace, so what it costs to be wrong is what governs: a verdict that could end a
#: working channel would take an agent off Slack over an inference nobody has watched, while one
#: that only says what it saw costs a line in a log. Readiness is Slack's `hello` and stays that.
ONE_APP = "the Socket Mode websocket and the bot token belong to the same Slack app"
TWO_APPS = ("the app-level token opened a Socket Mode websocket for a different Slack app than the "
            "bot token was issued by — Slack delivers an app's events only to that app's own "
            "connections, so nothing said to this bot can arrive here. Both tokens have to come "
            "from one app: the bot token from its OAuth & Permissions page, the app-level token "
            "from the same app's Basic Information page")
UNSURE_APP = ("could not establish whether the Socket Mode websocket and the bot token belong to "
              "one Slack app, so this is not being treated as either")

#: Message subtypes that are not somebody speaking. A join notice, an edit and a deletion all arrive
#: on the same event as a message does, and answering one is answering nothing. Most of these carry
#: no top-level `user` and would be refused anyway; they are named because a subtype that means
#: *this is not speech* should be refused for that reason and not by accident.
NOT_SPEECH = frozenset({
    "message_changed", "message_deleted", "message_replied", "bot_message", "tombstone",
    "channel_join", "channel_leave", "group_join", "group_leave", "channel_topic",
    "channel_purpose", "channel_name", "channel_archive", "channel_unarchive",
})

#: The same list, less the one word that is somebody speaking after all. **A peer agent's answer is
#: read as context and never as a wake-up**: an app posting with its own bot token may carry
#: `bot_message`, and dropping it from a thread being read for context is how a person who asks
#: "what do you think of that?" gets the question and not the answer it is about. Nothing here
#: widens what wakes the agent — `waking` refuses anything carrying a `bot_id`, whatever its subtype.
NOT_SAID = NOT_SPEECH - frozenset({"bot_message"})

#: How Slack spells a mention of exactly one person, and the only markup a turn's answer keeps
#: (R-SLK-15). **`U` and `W` both**: a workspace member is `U…` and Slack documents `W…` for a member
#: of an Enterprise Grid organisation, so a rule that knew only the first would escape a real
#: colleague's mention on exactly the installs where an agent has the most people around it.
#:
#: **The whole token and nothing near it.** Everything this does not match goes through `escaped`:
#: `<!channel>`, `<!here>`, `<!everyone>`, a user group's `<!subteam^S…>` and a channel link's
#: `<#C…>` each address more than one person, the deprecated `<@U…|label>` form carries a label
#: somebody else wrote, and a malformed one is not a mention at all. Bounded rather than open-ended,
#: so a line of angle brackets cannot become one enormous match.
A_MENTION = re.compile(r"<@[UW][A-Z0-9]{1,32}>")

#: What separates the pieces of the one string rundesk keeps a conversation under. Rundesk never
#: parses it; what matters is that the same exchange produces the same string every time, that a
#: thread and the channel around it are not the same conversation, and that **the workspace is in
#: it** — a bot reachable from two workspaces, or from an Enterprise Grid with the same channel id
#: in two of them, would otherwise fold two exchanges into one session.
IN = ":"

#: How a thread is read when a mention arrives inside one, how much of it is carried, and how much
